On 08.11.2012 20:04, derekt75 wrote:
> 
> Given how many wishes you usually wind up with at the end, perhaps it would
> be sensible to use wishes earlier?

Well, no. I would indeed wish for necessary things earlier if
possible, but when I need them most I usually just don't have
wishes available. And once I have wishes (usually not before
the Castle) I am mostly consolidated already, or wish for any
essential and still lacking item.

> 
> yes, it's a gamble that you wish for that HoB and the next thing you know
> you found a HoB on the ground.  but it seems you're almost never lacking
> for wishes, so maybe the game would be easier if you used more of them?

The difficult phase of the game is very early; at that point I
rarely have wishes.

The effect you describe, wishing for something to just find the
same item a few turns later, happens also later in the game. I
recall games where I completed my necessary stuff in the Castle
using wishes, just to find a few of those items below in Valley.
(The same could actually have happened in that ?EW lacking game;
I mentally had been close to wish for that scroll type before I
entered the last level in the Wizard's tower to find it.)

> [I've snipped the repetition of the probabilities]
> 
> Are your experiences with the RNG different?  Have you ever tried writing
> enchant weapon with a new marker and found yourself holding a (1:0) marker
> and no scroll of enchant weapon at the end of your effort?
> 
> If you do it often enough, sure the 3.5% is going to happen.  but I suspect
> you haven't tried it often enough to have seen that scenario play out.

The numbers are valid if you ask for the probabilities of the
_average_ of ones played games. But that view is (while helpful
for an estimate of odds of options you have) not sufficient if
I want to increase the chances in my concrete individual game.

And it's mostly not very relevant for my way of playing, where
I will always try to win each individual game, and I will try
to win without relying on luck or on absence of bad luck. For
example; with a [blessed] magic lamp you have an 80% chance for
a wish, a wish that may be crucial if you find it early. I would
take the risk (or chance) to get something only if I am in need
of some important item. But usually I will keep that source of
permanent light, because it increases my survival chances, thus
forgo the 80% chance of something that might be a desire to have.

I've far too often experienced "good odds" to work against me in
Nethack. So my motto is; avoid gambling if possible. I've already
explained upthread about the consideration of a real need in any
concrete situation; if gambling is crucial (i.e. I have no other
options left), well, then I gamble. If I have the option to not
gamble, I do that for a safe outcome. If I have options, but all
options require some gambling (i.e. if they are depending on the
likelihood how they work out in the given context), I will choose
the option that is most likely to solve the issue, and in case of
a quite equal likelihood I choose the option which might have a
strategic advantage later.

> 
> Have you ever used a new marker to reverse genocide dragons (when you knew
> how to write genocide) and found yourself holding a (1:0) marker with no
> dragon scales to show for your efforts?
> 
> (The chance that this has happened to anyone in the history of nethack is
